 External subroutine subprogram "samfdeepcnv_kh"
  (inc)(line-no.)(nest)(optimize)
               1                     !> \defgroup SAMF Scale-Aware Mass-Flux Deep Convection
               2                     !! @{
               3                     !!  \brief The scale-aware mass-flux (SAMF) deep convection scheme is an updated version of the previous Simplified Arakawa-Schubert (SAS) scheme with scale and aerosol awareness and parameterizes the effect of deep convection on the environment (represen
               4                     !!
               5                     !!  The SAS scheme uses the working concepts put forth in Arakawa and Schubert (1974) \cite arakawa_and_schubert_1974 but includes modifications and simplifications from Grell (1993) \cite grell_1993 such as saturated downdrafts and only one cloud type (t
               6                     !!
               7                     !! The SAMF scheme updates the SAS scheme with scale- and aerosol-aware parameterizations from Han et al. (2017) \cite han_et_al_2017 based on the studies by Arakawa and Wu (2013) \cite arakawa_and_wu_2013 and Grell and Freitas (2014) \cite grell_and_frei
               8                     !!
               9                     !!  \section diagram Calling Hierarchy Diagram
              10                     !!  \image html SAMF_Flowchart.png "Diagram depicting how the SAMF deep convection scheme is called from the FV3GFS physics time loop" height=2cm
              11                     !!  \section intraphysics Intraphysics Communication
              12                     !!  This space is reserved for a description of how this scheme uses information from other scheme types and/or how information calculated in this scheme is used in other scheme types.
              13                     
              14                     !> \file samfdeepcnv.f
              15                     !!  Contains the entire SAMF deep convection scheme.
              16                     
              17                     !>  \brief This subroutine contains the entirety of the SAMF deep convection scheme.
              18                     !!
              19                     !!  For grid sizes larger than threshold value, as in Grell (1993) \cite grell_1993 , the SAMF deep convection scheme can be described in terms of three types of "controls": static, dynamic, and feedback. The static control component consists of the simpl
              20                     !!
              21                     !! For grid sizes smaller than threshold value, the cloud base mass flux in the SAMF scheme is determined by the cumulus updraft velocity averaged ove the whole cloud depth (Han et al., 2017 \cite han_et_al_2017 ), which in turn, determines changes of the
              22                     !!
              23                     !!  \param[in] im number of used points
              24                     !!  \param[in] ix horizontal dimension
              25                     !!  \param[in] km vertical layer dimension
              26                     !!  \param[in] delt physics time step in seconds
              27                     !!  \param[in] delp pressure difference between level k and k+1 (Pa)
              28                     !!  \param[in] prslp mean layer presure (Pa)
              29                     !!  \param[in] psp surface pressure (Pa)
              30                     !!  \param[in] phil layer geopotential (\f$m^2/s^2\f$)
              31                     !!  \param[inout] ql cloud water (kg/kg)
              32                     !!  \param[inout] qi cloud ice (kg/kg)
              33                     !!  \param[inout] q1 updated tracers (kg/kg)
              34                     !!  \param[inout] t1 updated temperature (K)
              35                     !!  \param[inout] u1 updated zonal wind (\f$m s^{-1}\f$)
              36                     !!  \param[inout] v1 updated meridional wind (\f$m s^{-1}\f$)
              37                     !!  \param[out] cldwrk cloud workfunction (\f$m^2/s^2\f$)
              38                     !!  \param[out] rn convective rain (m)
              39                     !!  \param[out] kbot index for cloud base
              40                     !!  \param[out] ktop index for cloud top
              41                     !!  \param[out] kcnv flag to denote deep convection (0=no, 1=yes)
              42                     !!  \param[in] islimsk sea/land/ice mask (=0/1/2)
              43                     !!  \param[in] garea area of grid box (\f$m^2\f$)
              44                     !!  \param[in] dot layer mean vertical velocity (Pa/s)
              45                     !!  \param[in] ncloud number of cloud species
              46                     !!  \param[out] ud_mf updraft mass flux multiplied by time step (\f$kg/m^2\f$)
              47                     !!  \param[out] dd_mf downdraft mass flux multiplied by time step (\f$kg/m^2\f$)
              48                     !!  \param[out] dt_mf ud_mf at cloud top (\f$kg/m^2\f$)
              49                     !!  \param[out] cnvw convective cloud water (kg/kg)
              50                     !!  \param[out] cnvc convective cloud cover (unitless)
              51                     !!  \param[in] clam coefficient for entrainment rate
              52                     !!  \param[in] c0s convective rain conversion parameter (1/m)
              53                     !!  \param[in] c1 conversion parameter of detrainment from liquid water into grid-scale cloud water (1/m)
              54                     !!  \param[in] betal fraction factor of downdraft air mass reaching ground surface over land
              55                     !!  \param[in] betas fraction factor of downdraft air mass reaching ground surface over sea
              56                     !!  \param[in] evfact evaporation factor from convective rain
              57                     !!  \param[in] evfactl evaporation factor from convective rain over land
              58                     !!  \param[in] pgcon reduction factor in momentum transport due to convection induced pressure gradient force
              59                     !!  \param[in] asolfac aerosol-aware parameter inversely proportional to CCN number concentraion
              60                     !!
              61                     !!  \section general General Algorithm
              62                     !!  -# Compute preliminary quantities needed for static, dynamic, and feedback control portions of the algorithm.
              63                     !!  -# Perform calculations related to the updraft of the entraining/detraining cloud model ("static control").
              64                     !!  -# Perform calculations related to the downdraft of the entraining/detraining cloud model ("static control").
              65                     !!
              66                     !!  -# For grid sizes larger than the threshold value (currently 8 km):
              67                     !!  -# 1) Using the updated temperature and moisture profiles that were modified by the convection on a short time-scale, recalculate the total cloud work function to determine the change in the cloud work function due to convection, or the stabilizing ef
              68                     !!  -# 2) For the "dynamic control", using a reference cloud work function, estimate the change in cloud work function due to the large-scale dynamics. Following the quasi-equilibrium assumption, calculate the cloud base mass flux required to keep the lar
              69                     !!
              70                     !!  -# For grid sizes smaller than the threshold value (currently 8 km):
              71                     !!  -# 1) compute the cloud base mass flux using the cumulus updraft velocity averaged ove the whole cloud depth.
              72                     !!
              73                     !!  -# For scale awareness, the updraft fraction (sigma) is obtained as a function of cloud base entrainment. Then, the final cloud base mass flux is obtained by the original mass flux multiplied by the \f$(1-sigma)^2\f$.
              74                     !!  -# For the "feedback control", calculate updated values of the state variables by multiplying the cloud base mass flux and the tendencies calculated per unit cloud base mass flux from the static control.
              75                     !!  \section detailed Detailed Algorithm
